Keys are pre-declared with a type, and any attribute dictionary may contain values for any defined key. Keys are named (fully qualified), typed, and documented:

```
use std::time::Duration;
use hyperactor::attrs::Attrs;
use hyperactor::attrs::declare_key;
// Declare keys with their associated types
declare_key!(TIMEOUT, Duration, "Request timeout");
declare_key!(MAX_RETRIES, u32, "Maximum retry count");
let mut attrs = Attrs::new();
attrs.set(TIMEOUT, Duration::from_secs(30));
attrs.set(MAX_RETRIES, 3);
assert_eq!(attrs.get(TIMEOUT), Some(&Duration::from_secs(30)));
assert_eq!(attrs.get(MAX_RETRIES), Some(&3));
```

Attribute dictionaries can thus be serialized using any serde serializer, including YAML and JSON.

There are several uses of such attribute dictionaries, e.g.:

- Extensible configuration. Currently our configuration is hard-coded. This will allow us to use one configuration for the whole system, including base hyperactor, hyperactor subcrates, and monarch crates. We can now also override all values in a uniform manner.
- Message headers. We can define extensible message headers using attributes, providing an efficient, type-safe way to define, access, and transmit such metadata [1].
- Extensible specs, for example in the mesh allocators. With attributes, each allocator can specify a set of keys they support, and specs can be encoded as attr dicts which can be passed around in a uniform way -- with type-safety, uniform representation, etc.


[1] Here, we can also apply a representation trick: all headers can be reduced to a small hash. Because all keys are pre-registered, we can validate at runtime that there are no hash collisions.

